From: Loong <galletzeynep481@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: httpd: accept the bootloader too, and blink while writing

Three changes, all aimed at the same thing: making a first-time migration
survivable without a serial console.

  - The form now takes BL2 and the U-Boot FIP alongside the firmware, plus a
    "rebuild UBI" checkbox.  Without this a board that has never run this
    U-Boot needs two rounds of xmodem -- the first one only gets far enough
    to erase the flash before _firstboot resets it.  With it, one xmodem
    round brings up a RAM-resident U-Boot and everything else arrives over
    the network.  The parts are flashed in place, so a 64 MB upload still
    needs no second copy in RAM, and the bootloader is written before the
    firmware so a migration that dies halfway still leaves a recoverable
    board.

  - Flashing blinks all panel LEDs together instead of holding them solid.
    A solid panel is indistinguishable from a hung board, which is the one
    thing the LEDs exist to rule out.  net_loop()'s timeout handler is gone
    by then, so the blink is driven by the cyclic framework: the SPI-NAND
    layer calls schedule() once per written page, which keeps it ticking
    even in the middle of a multi-megabyte write.

  - A successful flash reboots on its own.  Previously it fell back into
    boot_httpd_forever and served the form again, which reads as "nothing
    happened".  A failed flash deliberately does not reboot, so the user
    gets the form back and can retry.

